From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:05:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] script: Add a fetcher of media resources for the media cache

The media cache fetches the bytes of a media resource through the
Fetcher trait, on any thread, and decides which ranges to request and
when to suspend, resume or cancel a fetch. Add the implementation of
that trait for media elements.

ScriptFetcher builds the request of a media element as the resource
fetch algorithm does: destination audio or video, a potential-CORS
request for the crossorigin attribute, the document as the client, and
the referrer policy of the element. Each fetch copies it and adds a
Range header for the byte where it starts and, for a request after the
first, If-Range with the validator of the first response. It starts the
fetch with fetch_async, so that the response arrives on the FetchThread
of the content process rather than on the script thread, and controls
it through a FetchController: a suspension carries a reply that tells
the cache whether net suspended the fetch, and cancelling or dropping
the handle aborts it.

The response is classified for the cache: whether it is a 206 and where
its body starts in the resource, the length of the resource from
Content-Range or Content-Length, its strong ETag or else its
Last-Modified date as the validator, whether it has a Content-Encoding,
under which byte positions and lengths do not apply, whether it is
CORS-cross-origin, and the origin of its final URL. A 416 ends the fetch as RangeNotSatisfiable, another
status that is not a success as Status, a request that CSP, mixed
content or a CORS check blocked as Blocked, and any other network error
as Network.

What only script can do is queued as tasks of the networking task source
on the element, which they do not keep alive: CSP violation reports,
resource timing entries for every fetch, and the failure steps of the
element when the first fetch fails before its response, so that a
request that its document blocks fails the element even if the cache
serves other readers.

The fetcher claims the blob of a blob URL for its lifetime, so that
restarts and gap fetches still work after the page revoked the URL, and
gives every fetch a token of its own (UrlWithBlobClaim::with_new_token),
since net ends the claim of a request's token once that request is
done. It accepts only a 200, or a 206 with a valid byte range, as
"verify a media response" asks, and answers a suspension that cannot
carry a reply as declined.

Nothing uses the fetcher yet; the next patch moves the media element to
the media cache.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: through the media element in the next patch.

Validation: `./mach check` and `./mach check --ohos` pass. See the next
patch for WPT and the scenarios.

AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; implemented and tested by the agent)
